        "gui": {
          "x": "640.0",
          "y": "220.0",
          "w": "300"
        },
        "parms": "1. Pick program 1 to 4 on your device. pgmin shows the program and the channel, and minus 1 recalls that preset slot, counted from 0: the tempo and cutoff boxes jump to the values the slot holds. Program 5 and up are slots this preset does not have, and nothing changes.",
        "type": ".text"
      },
      "object 26": {
        "ID": 26,
        "gui": {
          "x": "640.0",
          "y": "360.0",
          "w": "300"
        },
        "parms": "2. Send a SysEx message, or a dump from a synth. trigger hands each byte to zl group, then to sel 247: the closing byte bangs zl group, which sends the whole message to the list box. A long dump runs past the edge of the box.",
        "type": ".text"
      },
      "object 27": {
        "ID": 27,
        "gui": {
          "x": "640.0",
          "y": "550.0",
          "w": "300"
        },
        "parms": "3. Start a sequencer that sends MIDI clock to this port. rtin shows each byte. Start resets the counter and switches the toggle on, stop switches it off. The counter counts clock ticks, and divided by 24 they are quarter notes.",
        "type": ".text"
      },
      "object 28": {
        "ID": 28,
        "gui": {
          "x": "640.0",
          "y": "690.0",
          "w": "300"
        },
        "parms": "4. Continue, 251, leaves the count where stop left it, because only 250 resets it. Many sequencers keep sending ticks while they are stopped, so the count runs on: the toggle tells you whether one is playing.",
        "type": ".text"
      },
